{
 "_comment": "Prevailing-wage LEVEL promised on certified H-1B labour condition applications, by employer, pooled over the years listed. Level I is the entry rate for an occupation and Level IV the experienced one; filing at Level I or II is lawful and is also the cheapest legal way to fill a role. THIS IS WHAT WAS PROMISED, not what was paid. Cross-referenced against the DOL enforcement extract, which publishes only its LARGEST cases -- so 'in_enforcement_top' false means NOT IN THAT EXTRACT and is NOT EVIDENCE OF NO FINDINGS. THE CROSSING IS INCONCLUSIVE: six employers match, which cannot support a correlation in either direction. Enforcement is also not random: large employers and staffing firms are investigated more, and both skew low-level, so any association here CANNOT be read causally. Employers are grouped on the filed name with case and whitespace normalised only; nothing is fuzzy-matched. Employers with fewer than 50 filings are excluded because a handful of filings produces meaningless percentages.",
 "source": "US DOL Office of Foreign Labor Certification, H-1B LCA disclosure data",
